## 3. SQL Exploratory Analysis & Customer Segments

![Power BI Dashboard Preview](dashboards/dashboard.png)

Using **MS SQL Server**, ten core business questions (`Q1–Q10`) were analyzed:

1. **Discount Dependency vs. High-Value Promo Users (`Q2` & `Q6`):** While **Q2** isolates profitable promo users who spend above the store average (`$59.76`) when given a discount, **Q6** ranks the Top 5 products with the highest `discount_usage_rate`, exposing items that rely heavily on margin-diluting promotions without minimum cart gates.
2. **Loyalty & Repeat-Buyer Subscription Gap (`Q5`, `Q7` & `Q9`):** Using CTEs in **Q7** to segment buyers into `'New'` (`previous_purchases = 1`), `'Returning'` (`2–10`), and `'Loyal'` (`> 10`), alongside **Q9** (`previous_purchases >= 5` by `subscription_status`) and **Q5**, revealed that a massive cohort of proven repeat buyers (`73.0%` of the dataset) has not enrolled in the subscription program.
3. **Category Bestsellers & Top-Rated Upsell Candidates (`Q3` & `Q8`):** **Q3** identifies the Top 5 products by `avg_review_rating`, while **Q8** uses `DENSE_RANK()` to isolate the Top 3 most purchased items per category—providing a data-backed product feed for checkout upsell carousels.
4. **Demographic, Gender & Shipping Spend Drivers (`Q1`, `Q4` & `Q10`):** Evaluated revenue splits across `gender` (**Q1**) and `age_group` (**Q10**), while **Q4** compares average basket sizes between `'Standard'` and `'Express'` `shipping_type` to inform loyalty shipping perks.

